Polished English Version

Hello everyone,
I just want to share that I’ve officially earned my Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science.

I’m sorry if the video of me crying after my lecturer announced that I passed my final thesis defense looks a bit over the top. I was just overwhelmingly happy. My whole body was shaking.

I’ve loved computers for as long as I can remember—since junior high school. I knew early on that computer science was my passion. Unfortunately, my parents didn’t allow me to enter a computer-related major in high school, even though I cried so hard about it. I ended up spending three years in a major they chose for me.

Those years were honestly hell. I couldn’t find friends with the same interests, our personalities were completely different, and I felt very alone. I spent most of my time in the school library by myself.

After graduating from high school, my parents finally allowed me to study Computer Science—mainly because they saw how depressed I had become. When I entered college, I felt unbelievably happy, like I was finally in heaven. I found my people. I found my species.

But life had another challenge waiting.

After COVID, my family went bankrupt and could no longer afford my tuition. I told my mom, “Ma, I want to work while continuing college.” At first, she was worried I wouldn’t be able to focus. My dad even suggested that I stop studying altogether.

Thankfully, I had already been self-studying full-stack development through Udemy courses and YouTube tutorials ever since i got into college. I built several projects using the MERN stack (MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js)—yes, I was a total JavaScript baby 😄.

Finding a job was extremely hard. I got rejected dozens of times, mostly because I hadn’t graduated yet. I fell into depression again, but I survived because my mom never stopped supporting me. She always said, “Mama believes in you. Mama knows you best.”

She said that because she saw how hard I worked every single day—staying up late to code, building projects—while my father and grandparents often underestimated me. At the same time, I helped my parents sell vegetables and oranges on the side of the road.

Those words from my mom were the reason I never gave up.

One day, I saw an opening for a free bootcamp. At first, I hesitated because I wanted to work immediately, not spend months studying again. But my mom said, “It’s okay, just try. It’s free.”

So I applied—and thank God, I passed the tests and live coding.

I joined a 2-month Golang bootcamp. Out of 15 students, I ranked 1st, with an average grade of A, and became the Lead Backend Developer for my team of five. I had never ranked first in my entire life. I was so happy.

After graduation from bootcamp, there was a waiting period for job placement. The bootcamp staff said it might take a long time since I hadn’t graduated yet. But God had a different plan.

I became the first person from my batch to be onboarded.

I was so happy I couldn’t stop jumping, running, and hugging everyone around me. My grandparents were happy. My father was happy. My mom was happy. And most importantly, my younger siblings were incredibly proud of me.

As the eldest child, I’ve always wanted to be a good example for them. I don’t care what others think—I only care about my two siblings. I would do anything for them.

Then I moved to Jakarta, the capital of Indonesia. Walking into the office for the first time felt unreal. The building was luxurious beyond anything I had imagined. I was placed as an Application Developer at one of the largest state-owned banks in Indonesia.

I can never thank God enough.

I know I’m not perfect. I make mistakes, and I don’t always go to church. But I believe God sees everything—every night I cried, every moment I felt lonely. I believe God helped me because my intentions were never bad.

In Jakarta, I had no one. But my mom’s friend allowed me to stay in her house. She was incredibly kind. I didn’t even have proper work clothes, but she gave me her daughter’s old office clothes—still in great condition and branded.

This helped me so much, especially since I still had to pay my tuition. Renting a place in Jakarta was impossible for me.

Her house was close to my office. She often shared lunch with me and invited me to church every Sunday. I couldn’t give much in return, so I cooked for her sometimes.

One day, I cooked Soto Medan, because I missed my mom’s cooking. My mom’s Soto Medan is the best in the world—nothing can beat it. I learned how to make it by helping her for years—grinding the spices, stirring the coconut milk–based broth nonstop for 30 minutes, because if you stop, it can split and ruin the soup, and peeling hundreds of bean sprouts one by one.

Sometimes I think my perfectionist nature comes from my mom.

But honestly, it’s worth it—because the result tastes amazing. 

When (my mom's friend) tasted my cooking, she said it was five stars and asked me to cook it again. I was happy—but I still told her, “My mom’s is much better.” I missed my mom so much.

Eventually, I entered my final semester and had to work on my proposal and thesis. I chose a crazy topic: microservices, data science, and supply chain management—three fields in one thesis.

My lecturer doubted it and asked me to simplify. Instead, I built the project first.

I was overwhelmed. I worked full-time while building this insane project. I had zero experience with Java, yet I chose Java because I knew it was widely used in banking—and I wanted to master it. I had zero knowledge of data science, but I forced myself to learn.

I read Java documentation, studied OOP, researched supply chain management concepts, bought data science courses, and watched crash courses. I almost gave up, especially because of the math—statistics and probability that I had completely forgotten.

But I pushed through.

In the end, I completed a microservices-based supply chain system with data science integration. I even got sick with a fever for a week because of the stress.

My first proposal defense failed due to poor preparation. I was devastated. I rested for a few weeks, read novels, watched K-dramas, healed myself—and tried again.

And here I am.

A Bachelor of Computer Science.